1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
So let's talk about the systems manager

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,000
parameter store.

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
So it's a way for you to store configuration

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
and secrets securely on AWS.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
You can store anything you want, such as API keys,

6
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
passwords, configurations, and it's serverless.

7
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,000
That means you have nothing to provide.

8
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,000
It's scalable.

9
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
It can respond to many API calls all the time.

10
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
It's durable and it's very easy to use.

11
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,000
On top of it, it's secure

12
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
because you control access to each parameter

13
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
in the parameter store using IAM.

14
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
On top of it, your configurations can evolve,

15
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
your parameters can evolve

16
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,000
and so therefore you have version tracking

17
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
and optional encryption.

18
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,000
So we'll see in a second.

19
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
But in the parameter store, our applications

20
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
or our users can enter plain text configurations

21
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:43,000
or encrypted configurations

22
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
in which case it is encrypted with KMS.

23
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,000
And so therefore you can manage

24
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
and centrally store the configurations

25
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
of many of your applications in one place.

26
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
So I am in systems manager, and on the left hand side

27
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,000
there is the parameter store.

28
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
And as you'll see, it's extremely easy to use.

29
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
So going to create a parameter

30
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
and the name is going to be demo parameter.

31
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Then you can have a different tier, so standard or advanced.

32
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
So the free one is standard.

33
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
We'll use standard and then the type of parameter.

34
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,000
So is it a string

35
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
meaning it's a value that is, for example, a configuration

36
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
or a list of values if you wanted to, or a secure string.

37
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
And secure string is when you want to encrypt something

38
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,000
in the parameter store because you're, for example

39
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
going to add in API keys or passwords and so on.

40
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
So in this case, using a secure string is much better.

41
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
But for simplicity's sake, let's use a string.

42
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
And so you can choose the data type, so it could be a text

43
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
or an image of type EC2, but we'll use text and say, hey

44
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
the string is my configuration parameter.

45
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,000
And then that's it.

46
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
You just create this parameter and there you go.

47
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,000
In your parameter store

48
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
you have this parameter named demo parameter

49
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
that you can just click on

50
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
and then retrieve the value of value.

51
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
My configuration parameter on top of it.

52
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
If you were to edit it, there would be different versions

53
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
which is very helpful for tracking them over time.

54
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:08,000
And that's it.

55
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The parameter store is a very simple service

56
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
but a very helpful one in AWS.

57
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
When you're done, you can just delete this parameter

58
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
and you're back where you started.

59
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Okay, so that's it for this lecture.

60
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

